WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.310
Film Courage: Your first draft…how much
weight do you let it carry and then how much

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.530
time (you talk about giving time/space to
actually reading the story), doing other things,

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.480
trying to forget about it, then coming back
to it.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.710
What about the first draft?

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:17.650
Phyllis Nagy, Screenwriter of CAROL Movie:
In what do you mean, do I let it sit for awhile?

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.510
Film Courage: How much weight do you actually
give it or obsess over it…you know they

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.860
call it the vomit draft…

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.210
Phyllis Nagy: Yeah, I’m not a vomiter.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:35.390
And mostly I think that when scripts are not
right fundamentally in their first drafts,

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:42.760
they never will be, hence four, five, six
writers being called on to something in service

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.469
that wasn’t right to begin with.

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:53.489
So you should get a new take rather than try
to put a bandaid on something that doesn’t

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:54.489
work.

00:00:54.489 --> 00:01:01.300
I wish more weight and more time were given
to first drafts because they are often the

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:07.600
draft that’s closest to something electric
whether you’re writing a thriller or a quiet

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:16.220
little movie or a comedy and subsequent drafts
and however long someone works on something

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:23.210
in the end and it always ends up going back
to an earlier state when you’ve got the

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.040
right people working on something.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:32.009
It’s always (in my experience) goes back
to its first fundamental state with improvements.

